  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1203 AM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nThe upper level trough is forecast to continue to weaken and lift\nnortheast from AZ/UT into the southern/central Rockies Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday, forcing precipitation across western and \ncentral NM. The best chances will be across the northern mountains\nand RGV from Santa Fe to Taos, with relatively high snow levels \nof 8,500-9,500ft. Snow amounts Tuesday night into Wednesday will \nlikely be too low to warrant advisories, but the Tusas Mountains \nwould be the most likely candidate. High chances that this will \nbe a low-to-no impact precipitation event. Look for a break on \nWednesday behind the departing trough, with backing and increasing\nsouthwest flow on Thursday in advance of a potent upper level \ntrough/low moving inland along the west coast. The latest model \nsolutions have come into better agreement depicting the upper low\nfilling, opening up and racing east across the Great Basin into \nthe Rockies on Friday, bringing windy conditions to NM that may \npersist into Saturday. QPF with this system continues to dwindle \nwith each forecast cycle due to the deamplifying and progressive \nupper level trough. There is some notable model spread beyond \nFriday, but the weekend looks dry at this time.",
